#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116351} :activity/id #uuid "cf42fdc1-dee5-53a6-abe0-79ec4ef7b900" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420 :topic/id #uuid "9d6972e8-70d1-541f-8fd7-7896e8369046" :topic/math-academy-id 1691} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116507 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **differentiable** at a point $x=a$ if the derivative $f'(a)$ exists.\n\nIn general:\n\n- If a function is not continuous at some point, then it is not differentiable at that point either.\n\n- If a function is differentiable at some point, then it must be continuous at that point.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given a step function $y=f(x)$ that has a jump discontinuity at $x=3,$ given below. We will verify that $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3$ by showing that $f'(3)$ does not exist. \n\n![](images/9f/9f91b034a27ce7fd2b432f0b094e4b40098247916b78f9c1b3eb64c7214f69b4.png)\n\nThe value $f'(3)$ is a limit, so to check whether it exists, we need to compute the left and right-sided limits.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, we have that $f(x) = 2.$ So the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2-2}{x-3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, we have that $f(x)=1.$ So the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1-2}{x-3}={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{-1}{x-3}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}=\\text{DNE}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60845968-f9d5-58f2-9205-7c60bc949ed6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 332 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "62b549c4-9fa6-595c-9705-edecd57fab60" :step/math-academy-id 615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116354}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115380 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114920 :question/id #uuid "511342eb-691a-57be-a7cc-f10ab430b575" :question/math-academy-id "e-131"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341929}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "7d001457-3a19-5fb6-b582-e4447e917a71" :step/math-academy-id 616 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115381 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114921 :question/id #uuid "68eb6b1f-955c-550b-9232-3c6115ac220f" :question/math-academy-id "e-132"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392595}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "d3a114b6-40b8-5189-a0c2-30250448ba57" :step/math-academy-id 619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116514 :tutorial/content "If a function is continuous, that does *not* mean that it's differentiable!\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $f(x)=|x|.$ The function $f(x)$ is continuous everywhere, including the point $x=0.$ But is $f(x)$ also differentiable everywhere? \n\n![](images/18/18644551713e0c1d451cf27bd43f3cc8fc8d7064d7af79df3cd2070af782c25e.png)\n\nFor $x < 0,$ the graph consists of a downwards-sloping line with slope $-1.$ For $x > 0,$ the graph consists of an upward-sloping line with slope $1.$ So, the derivative of the absolute value function is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-1\\, & x<0, \\\\ 1\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the derivative of $f(x)=|x|$ does not exist at $x=0.$ This is because the limit of the slope as we approach $x=0$ from the left is not equal to the limit as we approach from the right.\n\nIn general, a continuous function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=a$ if the function $f(x)$ has any of the following features:\n\n- A **corner point** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x)=|x|$ at $x=0.$\n\n- A **cusp** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x) = \\sqrt{|x|}$ at $x=0.$\n\n- A **vertical tangent** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at $x=0.$\n\n![](images/28/28a6f3a424556e1e60e4959b03a5c38a1ff6a5d0efb110a0e5a4bfadc7072511.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0df8723a-c6d6-562d-8cde-b44a47dd70bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 335 :tutorial/title "Continuity Does Not Imply Differentiability"} :step/id #uuid "872a7f94-c8a5-5f55-a91f-39909aca384f" :step/math-academy-id 622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116352}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114922 :question/id #uuid "de600cc4-19fc-53a7-babc-fc387eff718c" :question/math-academy-id "e-133"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341931}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"} :step/id #uuid "4284ae9c-1207-5fcc-829e-569528302322" :step/math-academy-id 623 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116353}}] :activity/title "Continuity and Differentiability of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115458}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097407} :activity/id #uuid "ef8b70d7-53d9-5368-870b-fb3c6b4e76be" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048430 :topic/id #uuid "9eb245aa-f88f-5f14-8f09-1f18ad67f8ed" :topic/math-academy-id 1076} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097407 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097811 :tutorial/content "The trigonometric expression\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}R} \\sin( x + {\\color{red}\\phi})\n$$\n\nis called the **amplitude-phase form** of sine. Here, ${\\color{blue}R}$ is the **amplitude** and ${\\color{red}\\phi}$ is the **phase** of the sine function.\n\nThe sum or difference of a sine function and a cosine function with the same argument can always be written as a sine function in amplitude-phase form. That is to say, if $a$ and $b$ are positive real numbers, then the expression\n\n$$\na \\sin x \\pm b \\cos x\n$$\n\ncan be written in the form \n\n$$\nR \\sin(x \\pm \\phi).\n$$\n\nfor some $R>0$ and $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "69964ee4-693c-5a43-af92-88232e3d1471" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2572 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "88224b15-9a6d-5755-a5b2-af292977df30" :step/math-academy-id 11889 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097812 :tutorial/content "We've been using the amplitude-phase form for sine, but there is also an amplitude-phase form for cosine. If $a$ and $b$ are positive real numbers, then the expression\n\n$$\na \\cos x \\pm b \\sin x\n$$\n\ncan be written in the form \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}R} \\cos(x \\mp {\\color{red}\\phi} )\n$$\n\nfor some ${\\color{blue}R}>0$ and ${\\color{red}\\phi} \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$ \n\nNote that the sign in the last formula flips due to the identity $\\cos(x \\mp \\phi) = \\cos x \\cos \\phi \\pm \\sin x \\sin \\phi,$ which reverses the sign when expanding.\n\nWriting this more explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\operatorname{cos}x+b\\operatorname{sin}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) \\\\ a\\operatorname{cos}x-b\\operatorname{sin}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's write the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sin{x}+\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nin amplitude-phase form with cosine. We start by expanding out the amplitude-phase form using the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ\\operatorname{cos}x+R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\operatorname{sin}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}x+(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the phase $\\phi.$ Equating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the first equation by the second one, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}ϕ & =4 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}(4) \\\\  & \\approx 1.326\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}ϕ+{cos}^{2}ϕ) & =16+1 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =17 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =\\sqrt{17}\\operatorname{cos}(x-\\operatorname{arctan}(4)) \\\\  & \\approx \\sqrt{17}\\operatorname{cos}(x-1.326)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e3bb9db1-8b71-5f57-8cd5-5a113632e7d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2574 :tutorial/title "The Amplitude-Phase Form For Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "d14d2ddb-2f8f-5f82-8559-27101b7b4ca4" :step/math-academy-id 11921 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097412}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097409 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096606 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096406 :question/id #uuid "6010c0f3-67d3-5b21-af26-695a864fb42a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3541"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406578}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"} :step/id #uuid "3819f187-e5ca-5e24-8560-2b9695d3ec9d" :step/math-academy-id 11924 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097411}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097410 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096607 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096407 :question/id #uuid "91e6008e-5c37-55aa-8cf6-cac61198f459" :question/math-academy-id "e-3542"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049773}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375398}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"} :step/id #uuid "f1336a15-53ea-5387-ae8a-f35eef5dcb59" :step/math-academy-id 11925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097409}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097411 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096608 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096408 :question/id #uuid "557b1b6e-c54a-5fa9-85e1-3cea346c3bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3543"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374656}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"} :step/id #uuid "6c1dd27c-aafa-514b-8675-0cf06d6d9413" :step/math-academy-id 11928 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097408}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097412 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096609 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096409 :question/id #uuid "9ea36397-ab9d-5567-8b38-3e83ba624e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3544"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048319}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406579}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "a827daaf-41a5-5e6d-be23-699162ae1f94" :step/math-academy-id 11929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097825 :tutorial/content "Let's write the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sin{x}+\\sqrt{3}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nin amplitude-phase form. We start by expanding out the amplitude-phase form using the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ\\operatorname{sin}x+R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x & =R\\operatorname{sin}(x+ϕ) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x & =(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}x+(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the phase $\\phi$. Equating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first one, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}ϕ+{sin}^{2}ϕ) & =9+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =12 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin{x}+\\sqrt{3}\\cos{x} = {\\color{blue}2\\sqrt{3}} \\sin \\left(x + {\\color{red}\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}} \\right)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "81c76ef1-c149-53cf-a9e2-d93a243a7002" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2887 :tutorial/title "Writing a Sum or Difference of Sine and Cosine Using Amplitude-Phase Form"} :step/id #uuid "d77693ed-4059-5f7a-88e9-25300c5994e9" :step/math-academy-id 15506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097410}}] :activity/title "Writing Sums of Trigonometric Functions in Amplitude-Phase Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096867}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406582} :activity/id #uuid "e1be1a0c-7a7b-5288-bec9-333df360e987" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048430 :topic/id #uuid "9eb245aa-f88f-5f14-8f09-1f18ad67f8ed" :topic/math-academy-id 1076} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f6be78-4a73-582f-b9be-ccd54037b7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406560} {:answer/id #uuid "4493693b-c02e-5ba8-9c1f-0e602a8b0eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406562} {:answer/id #uuid "9d920b62-e442-5621-8fa9-46a7ba4002db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406565} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a70056-20f6-5d8c-bd45-fe08a274eba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406569} {:answer/id #uuid "dac58597-74ad-5c55-82b7-a592a5d0742b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{70}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406562} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1825133-7014-5a09-9154-148509062f75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79089117-3e5a-51ff-ae41-40a7e2901f6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-65578" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a06f20a6-d2e4-56ba-a165-46b7fcfbdf95" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008d940c-e694-5cf3-8295-360b6cad867c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406559} {:answer/id #uuid "bf267273-4455-5b6e-9339-b99f0be93f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406567} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c281e4-9095-5e8a-ba0f-aabb9383d4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406568} {:answer/id #uuid "d474685d-0b20-52ae-90c4-81e995b0ce85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406570} {:answer/id #uuid "f607a11b-899b-59ab-b189-957fb6465093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406572}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406570} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4d9909-2b00-5980-9b7e-95f491417da2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "139292f5-27c6-5b2f-8f13-1b15372d0a1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65581" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =3 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cc02456f-a095-5e41-95ce-6a946f125439"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283fcbb3-8865-526f-9a34-cc2afbcdadd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406561} {:answer/id #uuid "641d46cb-ca02-51d3-a8b9-699328a21c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406563} {:answer/id #uuid "709cd0f7-451f-5ee9-8074-0e603e78ea3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406564} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a219de-60fc-550f-9a26-7578926916c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406566} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0bccfb-38e4-51f1-8863-481369f3db5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406573}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406561} :answer-field/id #uuid "83d8f167-e779-56fd-836e-ab6aac4f2070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e58d0138-9eca-5984-8b18-90ecf02f0d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-11294" :question/problem "$3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =18 \\\\ R & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fe07e852-ca7d-55ad-9fb8-dab24d09b6c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406580}}] :activity/title "Writing Sums of Trigonometric Functions in Amplitude-Phase Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406558}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116029} :activity/id #uuid "f25176a6-c4ec-5e06-958a-d147380e3a3e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048533 :topic/id #uuid "a7996653-9e76-59d9-b493-05cb731632cb" :topic/math-academy-id 1566} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116465 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\sqrt{2},  \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.\n$$\n\nMost calculators don't have an $\\textrm{arccsc}$ button, so we can't get a principal value immediately. However, by rewriting the equation in terms of $\\sin x,$ we can get a principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}x & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual. Our principal value is\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right)= 45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1=45^\\circ.$ It is in the first quadrant and has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/50/50f7efb47ec8c6dab76c82b77806b419a88b3944266346e32a8fefb05f5bd479.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\nx_2 = 180^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 135^\\circ .\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=45^\\circ, 135^\\circ.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0adfde9e-d7bb-5670-8b70-7eacc327744a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2566 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a6c284b1-1430-5bc1-980c-2779380bb0fa" :step/math-academy-id 11857 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116030}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115182 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115011 :question/id #uuid "a1fb7543-873c-5f53-af2b-3f9cf476864f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3533"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145bdcf2-b1d5-54a8-bd95-4b054d7a33f0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387015}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Special Angles"} :step/id #uuid "079c956e-37f6-595c-8f57-7cb89a4b1289" :step/math-academy-id 11858 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116466 :tutorial/content "The same method also works for solving an equation involving the cosecant function over a modified domain. Let's solve\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\sqrt{2}, \\qquad -360^\\circ < x < 360^\\circ.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt 2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find a principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right)= 45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${\\color{red}x_1}=45^\\circ.$ It is in the first quadrant and has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/50/50f7efb47ec8c6dab76c82b77806b419a88b3944266346e32a8fefb05f5bd479.png)\n\nSince the second solution $\\color{blue}x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x_2} = 180^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 135^\\circ\n$$\n\nHowever, we're not done yet. The domain $-360^\\circ < x < 360^\\circ$ is larger than one period, so there are more than two solutions.\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the desired domain, we need to find angles that are coterminal with ${\\color{red}x_1}$ or ${\\color{blue}x_2}.$ So, we subtract integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until all possible solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={x}_{1}-{360}^{∘}={45}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{315}^{∘} \\\\ {x}_{4} & ={x}_{2}-{360}^{∘}={135}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding or subtracting any more multiples of $360^\\circ$ would result in solutions outside the desired domain. So, we have found all the solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x= 45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, -315^\\circ, -225^\\circ.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c658ece9-e7a5-521c-bfa8-397147718c68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2567 :tutorial/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain"} :step/id #uuid "8c118a14-2cd3-5818-8575-ff726f0c00fe" :step/math-academy-id 11860 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115183 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115012 :question/id #uuid "ef65c632-0174-5dcf-aacf-40ec3e73e244" :question/math-academy-id "e-3535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395811}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain"} :step/id #uuid "fe94ae10-d477-5a1b-afac-d4c5e81cdcdc" :step/math-academy-id 11861 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115221 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115051 :question/id #uuid "5044ded5-04bb-57f8-935c-a4a9264d5055" :question/math-academy-id "e-4262"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9ccf6b5-e087-5f7b-88e3-3767efbdcc26" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345317}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Non-Special Angles"} :step/id #uuid "05b5117f-57e3-5f8e-a3cc-4275f23bb4c5" :step/math-academy-id 15644 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116485 :tutorial/content "Can we find any solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}, \\qquad  0 \\leq x < 2\\pi?\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x}= \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 2.\n$$\n\nThis equation has no solution because we always have $-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq 1,$ and the number $2$ is outside of this range. Therefore, $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ has no solution.\n\nIn general, when $-1 < a < 1$, there are no values of $x$ such that $\\csc x = a.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "65dc503f-e259-5203-94c5-915dbfdfb16e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2898 :tutorial/title "Equations Involving the Cosecant Function With No Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "765645e0-c6eb-531a-9b36-fa7997947a86" :step/math-academy-id 15651 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115222 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115052 :question/id #uuid "2ff5c81b-6278-5259-963b-8034c690e26c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4263"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049075}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403912}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"} :step/id #uuid "e5c624b2-d1ce-5480-9efb-0a47f727ca7d" :step/math-academy-id 15652}] :activity/title "Elementary Trigonometric Equations Containing Cosecant" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115412}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097522} :activity/id #uuid "b8439109-03d0-56d9-8a44-f5a5b744e4b7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742 :topic/id #uuid "ba72c50b-60dc-50dc-ae9b-3cc018d38d60" :topic/math-academy-id 1049} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097843 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/7c/7c2283c7de572acfc58b443499598554ecf3d26b1a13c48c80b8d6aadf7b576a.svg)\n\nThe matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form, but we can transform it even further into the **reduced row echelon form** (RREF) using the standard **Gauss-Jordan** method. \n\nThe algorithm is similar to the algorithm of converting a matrix to row echelon form, except that we need to make *all* the pivot entries equal to $1$ and *all* the entries below *and above* a pivot (in a pivot column) equal to $0.$\n\nIn our case, we convert $M$ to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final matrix is in reduced row echelon form:\n\n- all the pivot entries are equal to $1$ (here we have $\\color{red}1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- all the entries below and above a pivot are equal to ${\\color{blue}0}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ce056e74-9707-52d3-b172-483ca14e1f8f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3160 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "78885948-fa6f-5f46-8f96-e5da861960dd" :step/math-academy-id 17209 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097844 :tutorial/content "For a $3\\times 3$ system with $3$ pivots, the Gauss-Jordan method can be described as follows:\n\n- First, reduce the matrix to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination method (also making all pivot entries equal to $1$). \n![](images/5c/5c0f5ac3d95843b142c90e8ce83378ea2b2ad693baba6670e0cde46a449f5225.svg)\n\n- We use the third row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{33}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n- if $a_{23} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-a_{23})R_3$, and\n\n- if $a_{13} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{13})R_3$.\n\n- We use the second row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{22}$ equal to $0$. Namely, \n\n- if $a_{12} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{12})R_2$.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to reduced row echelon form.\n\n![](images/9c/9c817628a3c646a928f12548a109b8136bb0a5977fab6e4d18e972690d81159b.svg)\n\n**Note:** Unlike the row echelon form, the *reduced* row echelon form of a matrix is unique." :tutorial/id #uuid "f74e6075-9c2a-5495-ab21-470711559f2a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3161 :tutorial/title "General Algorithm (RREF)"} :step/id #uuid "278dc07d-f5da-5098-a755-d5a72662e2ac" :step/math-academy-id 17210 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097524}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096461 :question/id #uuid "de738faf-e020-5058-b6a5-48020212fbc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4709"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7294399b-b139-5ee6-824c-5e28e4fc49ff" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416435}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a 3x3 System That Is Already in Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "7f602ae0-3af8-588f-bdb5-c0995e19908f" :step/math-academy-id 17211 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096462 :question/id #uuid "239a008b-4c67-5334-8285-3c46b773fead" :question/math-academy-id "e-4710"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95a32907-8304-5dfd-8759-9e91503445bf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393410}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a 3x3 System That Is Not in Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "c80be1fb-c96f-5733-8d79-5c777f840ef9" :step/math-academy-id 17212 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096546 :question/id #uuid "283e9a4a-443f-579f-b272-248446d3a49f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b010a518-7aa3-5b53-9a6d-ef0779ff0dfa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422479}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With Two Pivots"} :step/id #uuid "f3553e51-447b-5d4a-b387-1bc43d2a825d" :step/math-academy-id 23346 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096547 :question/id #uuid "8de46974-719c-5ac2-a43c-83d1c67c57b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6234"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa91091b-5368-5d80-b87d-08b3524177f3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422478}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With One Pivot"} :step/id #uuid "4208bcd7-ee35-5977-8afd-7beb404e8b75" :step/math-academy-id 23347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097869 :tutorial/content "When a $3\\times3$ system of equations has two pivot columns instead of three, we can still reduce its augmented matrix to reduced row echelon form. The process is similar to what we did before. \n\n- First, reduce the matrix to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination method (also making all pivot entries equal to $1$). \n ![](images/99/992cfaabdb3490e99553fbbdfee0089b399306e3e65720fc6a70014bfa35321a.svg)\n\n- We use the second row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{22}$ equal to $0$. Namely, \n\n- if $a_{12} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{12})R_2$.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to reduced row echelon form.\n\n![](images/3d/3da5751a339ff2ee85d424b42a72f5270f1be076407a33abd1b79e0f082639d5.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "baed23fb-560e-56ad-aea3-46f3728ebfff" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3949 :tutorial/title "Using the Gauss-Jordan Method on a 3x3 System With Two Pivots"} :step/id #uuid "e4f6891c-3e25-562a-900b-9617b1000f05" :step/math-academy-id 23416 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097634}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097870 :tutorial/content "It's also possible to write the augmented matrix for a $3\\times 3$ system of linear equations with only one pivot in reduced row echelon form. \n\nLet's see an example of how this works." :tutorial/id #uuid "e3748ee3-9d5d-5a6a-808f-ef8f99cbd1aa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3950 :tutorial/title "Using the Gauss-Jordan Method on a 3x3 System With One Pivot"} :step/id #uuid "541dad41-3dc8-5018-acca-4d79ebbbda41" :step/math-academy-id 23417 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097635}}] :activity/title "Reduced Row Echelon Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096857}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422481} :activity/id #uuid "fda0e0b8-3081-5e52-8593-c6700e3052bb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742 :topic/id #uuid "ba72c50b-60dc-50dc-ae9b-3cc018d38d60" :topic/math-academy-id 1049} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee5bd89-c4e9-5aea-ba6a-3f045af5c0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422467} {:answer/id #uuid "75130a5b-9607-5217-b80c-6757c94daf70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422471} {:answer/id #uuid "8076b85b-1614-5135-bb4c-2ab5d41377da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422472} {:answer/id #uuid "d64479b7-f216-5594-be33-53f9d09c2c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422473} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ebe9c7-57ca-5f13-b184-b18fa5a512d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422472} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4a2cf38-1593-52f1-9b63-551383c8a50f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "df6c8a6d-153a-5484-848d-1043ee95c582" :question/math-academy-id "q-74621" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x-11y+5z=-1 \\\\ x-3y+2z=1 \\\\ -3x+9y-6z=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -11 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -11 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -11 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "06ed1b32-abf8-5d02-b409-e83aa1a9bf69" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422482}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f53140c-1654-5c33-b318-d2cc5cdbf3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416405} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8ed053-8c5f-5809-b29d-46bc30062dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416409} {:answer/id #uuid "52ed3b97-c09d-5bac-bedf-d8cb30fd9295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,-3,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416411} {:answer/id #uuid "ad204329-d0a2-52cb-bab5-d155cba3473f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-3,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416423} {:answer/id #uuid "fa5e31d8-6742-527d-a027-3e41d829bbd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-3,-2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416428}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416409} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9c6d72-c8a0-5fe2-aea3-51adff0f94d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb5d2e37-01c6-52dc-86a3-0e3d22b79684" :question/math-academy-id "q-20267" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+z=2 \\\\  & x+2y+z=0 \\\\  & x+y+2z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=3 \\\\  & y=-2 \\\\  & z=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=3$, $y=-2$, and $z=1.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨3,-2,1⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "1f437206-fa08-5382-b319-4b882827b9a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422483}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083caf03-1501-5cba-8190-446a66b010a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,3,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263075} {:answer/id #uuid "9af8bfe2-063c-5dba-b504-5816b98f9766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,-3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264325} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b913e9-5eb2-5d55-9bc3-2f3b88be543b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,-3,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264598} {:answer/id #uuid "da1a8356-2e88-513c-b7fa-151c2d4478c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,-2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264902} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e3c0bf-feab-56d8-8788-d7833ba1031b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264325} :answer-field/id #uuid "71bd9945-c16d-52b1-9144-9035edc43bba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8ff5175c-29cf-5e1c-8b8a-50a809738a87" :question/math-academy-id "q-131618" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-y+2z=5 \\\\  & 2x-y+7z=7 \\\\  & x-y+3z=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=2 \\\\  & y=-3 \\\\  & z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=2$, $y=-3$, and $z=0.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨2,-3,0⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "6862e7b5-f79b-5b64-a612-6d85862752ef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f1079e-5752-5de3-a9e1-e19c06fada97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339766} {:answer/id #uuid "599056ef-e9ef-5488-965a-9dd9e4140202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339942} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e2972b-b0c5-5496-8789-531b77f4e97c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340614} {:answer/id #uuid "bac13010-faae-5ec6-a632-601054299d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340802} {:answer/id #uuid "f4632778-f58f-50b4-b85c-9458c6830a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340614} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5965582-0f9c-5d43-87a1-fef40d8e1c11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a1acb1c2-35d8-56f8-bcad-3b6d0c4f6e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-74601" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+2y-z=3 \\\\  & -2x-3y+2z=0 \\\\  & 2x+3y-z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6e7d6a76-cc67-5b59-8e1b-d53698eacff0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422480}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7564c8-04d6-57a1-9a06-28d7b8421a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y+2z+2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422466} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1f7437-d98a-5bd7-97a0-2e5ff4f2f649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422468} {:answer/id #uuid "41cef48b-98a3-54bd-b406-d5e167d64ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2-y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422469} {:answer/id #uuid "45df3fe7-8c4e-5624-afa2-3e1ab32743c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y-2z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422470} {:answer/id #uuid "d82b73e6-0a25-5302-99e8-b157a818667e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422470} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d6b9331-1da4-5d89-9d4a-e8d26ffe05e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "86e15f08-648b-5bcc-988f-7551732eb466" :question/math-academy-id "q-131649" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+2y-4z=4 \\\\ -x+y-2z=2 \\\\ x-y+2z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z=-2 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=y-2z-2 \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}y-2z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9f34ebb7-b2e8-595a-840d-d43ea1cd168d"}] :activity/title "Reduced Row Echelon Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422465}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106673} :activity/id #uuid "e8bbfc2d-b5f4-57c0-9027-1b66b844109b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819 :topic/id #uuid "c29ac9c3-f2c1-572b-9251-f82101c5a30e" :topic/math-academy-id 1375} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106673 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107090 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A,$ an **eigenvector** of $A$ is a *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor some scalar $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The scalar $\\lambda$ is called the **eigenvalue** corresponding to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n![](images/63/63e2c65a23a0680412a14084d543b6cc19c0529fd80863b09c99987d309eaa4f.svg) \nand the vector ![](images/b7/b7811b74d8d3075357925864b5be27f57b292dadca9c04d222954baa1e66ba2a.svg) Computing the product $A \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe've discovered that $A {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = {\\color{red}-2} {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$ Therefore,\n\n- the vector ![](images/13/13a28e543647ede7a88b30fb652bd7fba6c9adb8ee3142161a102de947ef2d2a.svg) is an *eigenvector* of $A,$ and\n\n- the scalar $\\color{red}\\lambda=-2$ is the *eigenvalue* corresponding to the eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$\n\n**Caution:** Remember that an eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector. This is very important." :tutorial/id #uuid "1014a8e8-38ce-568b-86d7-22f32ef8f697" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3557 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9b7139a7-3f19-5d72-857b-7a01a291e46b" :step/math-academy-id 20075 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105768 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105620 :question/id #uuid "61bb0d60-a61e-5485-9df5-308e16dfd1aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5476"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411285}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"} :step/id #uuid "4921a5cc-f26d-5b7a-9865-e447eec1d53e" :step/math-academy-id 20077 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105621 :question/id #uuid "f4ff15d3-1a6e-56c0-a7c4-13f18ef1a8e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5477"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411286}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"} :step/id #uuid "64cdc019-4015-5867-b014-691bcc52330a" :step/math-academy-id 20078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105622 :question/id #uuid "6cba45d6-5a69-5b83-937c-c19368aaeea9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5478"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369017}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "6e9a9912-8def-5a76-892f-0608e119d89c" :step/math-academy-id 20081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105787 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105628 :question/id #uuid "df40f37e-0d8f-5eb2-ad8a-9257440851df" :question/math-academy-id "e-5891"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393556}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "5b97f2fc-3025-5372-9dc4-19c05d084b76" :step/math-academy-id 21410 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107098 :tutorial/content "Eigenvectors and eigenvalues can be interpreted geometrically.\n\nFirst, recall that if the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is the standard matrix of some linear transformation ![](images/80/80d488fc9d8168cecdf882f1aa178325bb36bd387dd9f86f2b0cd98a5c54f4dc.svg) then the product ![](images/dc/dcc52971e9a7e86ebbe8bf972c562fce13d389f9261b69cba05dcabb1426b9dc.svg) gives the image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/a1/a1af41bdb2f2fe410c6f8e02d1a2a0048f0aa470ceef4508753090444ca039be.svg) The image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) can appear quite different from the original vector ![](images/92/9221d4dc55c1df8da019956ea23f9d4f9ff3f5b04921ec2158804a31a50e636b.svg)\n\nHowever, for an eigenvector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/4a/4a2f3891f0b4c8b852767d556e1607028035219b70ab83b4910e95159a930b9c.svg) we get\n\n![](images/ee/eed93a562368727b490c2b63d14f46580dcd0439ac90df23a23f75a31de9e971.svg)\n\nIn other words, when the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is applied to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the image of ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is a scaled version of the original vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg), and the scale factor is ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f542f8920ca90af5418.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider once again the following matrix:\n\n![](images/43/43e76f5a5e889ec18a6893be8cdbf4837d3dedfab149a0981ce6619a62718170.svg)\n\nWe've already seen that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) has an eigenvector ![](images/fe/fef256ade4f52003da67fac5af68ff4b0d4e7f04ef55e72bb0279bdc8f1e8c3c.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/b5/b5cdbd2f286044241daca2565a1ab7f70196ecc2cf6503965d9a820faad9b06e.svg) So we have\n\n![](images/2d/2da94bfdde0e3650ded2622748ead5a4c09cabf610f9d181d48ae158d9bd0986.svg)\n\nPlotting the vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) and its image ![](images/ab/abe8027351c1645f4c582440b8b536f48f3ec35d008570f3432f322fa468e861.svg) in the plane, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/9a/9aca41e0597b0b1698e8ec24da9966fd9b7e5fee3951081d8abdb0cc0caaac7b.png)\n\nIn general, when we apply a matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the resulting vector ![](images/be/befecb9d885ee3bbbb7313401ca3671428e4aced68a4bfdb4b2fc9a005ea2b43.svg) always lies on the same line as ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)\n\nIf ![](images/8c/8c453358dacec49655b560be52a99066e3cab1d34cfbdbdbaa7bd74d868fef82.svg) then the matrix stretches the vector. For ![](images/ea/ea6d742051f82ff2f258e08ed09c3b3dc6d4c7f9bbbe40fea718aec8183dc44f.svg) the matrix compresses the vector. In addition, a negative eigenvalue ![](images/c9/c91cda6703c5767aaafeca807b07e466f160cb0ade84472ade5fa85d71a5713e.svg) means that ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is flipped in the opposite direction as well as scaled." :tutorial/id #uuid "cdf6ca75-e37b-552a-a106-ee8ac2f36a61" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3963 :tutorial/title "The Geometric Interpretation of Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"} :step/id #uuid "ab53090e-96b8-5dcc-b77f-6ff038689cac" :step/math-academy-id 23514 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106711}}] :activity/title "The Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106002}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106635} :activity/id #uuid "0211c399-b12f-5dd5-8ac4-97409f9a60bc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048853 :topic/id #uuid "c62c1f45-0d31-598a-b3ca-31da6d205f4d" :topic/math-academy-id 1373} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107080 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n![](images/6a/6a69daf7712312876b0ec97c540bbc472e46a7938beb8a59e694a04ad0b1d8c6.svg)\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/a8/a8bfe3f6b27247ee9b27db8346fd94fb9068c85dec6f4a71517a821dee48dbf3.svg)\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 2y = -5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we describe the **general solution** of our system.\n\n- The first variable in our equation, $x,$ is called a **basic variable**.\n\n- The second variable in our equation, $y,$ is called a **free variable**.\n\nSolving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x = -5+2y.$ So the general solution of the system is\n![](images/0b/0ba4a232f29dd6fce2a8f6d7cda39b4f5b192c20fc91fe4403dfe648231382c5.svg)\nwhere the free variable $y$ is any number.\n\nIn vector form, this solution is written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5+2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is the equation of a straight line on a plane. Any point on this line satisfies our system of equations.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $y$ and find the corresponding value of $x$ on the line. For example, if we choose $y=0,$ then the corresponding value is $x=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-5,$ $y=0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0c91df0b-40c7-5a65-9fda-5d2d6438dfa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3153 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2b66da6f-e7b3-547c-b47a-3bfe17bbc205" :step/math-academy-id 17192 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105602 :question/id #uuid "7bfabbd2-9988-5c0e-9c17-3d430b3e7a0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4699"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97cd9132-458b-5234-a3d2-22b633acb74c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414184}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Augmented Matrix for a Singular 2x2 System to REF and Solving the Resulting System"} :step/id #uuid "414f018d-78f3-5c78-86b8-6363d86d3a9e" :step/math-academy-id 17193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105603 :question/id #uuid "681fce57-6be7-5907-b575-8c34b4819ae5" :question/math-academy-id "e-4700"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07f2ae4c-ff07-5b99-a7b0-90f460081eef" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414186}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Singular 2x2 System of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "00d5123d-64a7-5878-9b67-ac7b7a2bf0a0" :step/math-academy-id 17194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107081 :tutorial/content "Given a $2 \\times 2$ system of linear equations, we have the following possibilities for the number of solutions:\n\n- The system has exactly *one solution* if and only if the respective coefficient matrix is non-singular (meaning that the determinant is not zero). Such systems are called **consistent independent**.\n\n- The system has *infinitely many solutions* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s. These systems are called **consistent dependent**.\n\n- The system has *no solution* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s on the left-hand side and a non-zero element on the right-hand side. These are called **inconsistent** systems.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\n**Note:** A system of linear equations of the form \n![](images/cb/cb397dd01794f28ff11c01079e287d63175e439c8bbaa85cd02dc61689780fa8.svg)\nwhere we have only $0$'s on the right-hand side, is called **homogeneous**. A homogeneous system always has at least one solution, namely, the solution $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b885f832-9e76-5008-a764-7375b1c25e35" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3154 :tutorial/title "The Number of Solutions of a 2x2 System"} :step/id #uuid "dc00947a-2ca5-59a7-a7a8-349b27d59bf2" :step/math-academy-id 17195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107097 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, a system of equations might not have any solutions. For example, consider the following augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is\n![](images/e8/e8c64d8be247afd3e8ab02abc69e33635ef0234dbcbbc60f42379021a39a58a2.svg)\n\nThe final equation, $0x+0y=10,$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=10,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "20c281f5-a455-581a-a7f7-fec6cfaa114e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3901 :tutorial/title "Singular Systems with No Solution"} :step/id #uuid "e6eff47b-880f-55d6-94ac-af8b9767c4fd" :step/math-academy-id 23052 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106637}}] :activity/title "Solving 2x2 Singular Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106000}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106952} :activity/id #uuid "e36ee21c-371b-5637-8539-624dc138ba57" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048880 :topic/id #uuid "c9449775-49cd-5723-869d-965280e34916" :topic/math-academy-id 1382} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107173 :tutorial/content "Consider an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty, \\infty),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent." :tutorial/id #uuid "b58359c2-9f54-5bd7-8005-df9313559853" :tutorial/math-academy-id 932 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e2a6a97c-d17d-5e17-9d7b-d0d1dbe224ca" :step/math-academy-id 618349 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106953}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105608 :question/id #uuid "dbd8b706-9ace-5adb-9c8e-29b0d21bfa82" :question/math-academy-id "e-515"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051015}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351298}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"} :step/id #uuid "61313614-0ee3-57e5-8e4b-da016cba1b7b" :step/math-academy-id 618350 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106954}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107099 :tutorial/content "Computing the definite integral of a function over the entire real line requires us to break the integral into two parts and separately evaluate each one.\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating two improper integrals can be quite time-consuming. However, we can shorten the process if we see that $f(x)$ is an even or odd function. \n\n- Recall that $f(x)$ is an even function if $f(-x) = f(x).$ Even functions are symmetrical about the $y$-axis. Therefore, if $f(x)$ is even, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nprovided that the integrals are convergent. Moreover, if $f(x)$ is even and the above integrals are convergent, then\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = 2\\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n- Recall that $f(x)$ is an odd function if $f(-x) = -f(x).$ Therefore, if $f(x)$ is odd, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = -\\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nprovided that the integrals are convergent. Moreover, if $f(x)$ is odd and the above integrals are convergent, then\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "04e63304-93d0-5edd-985c-6a732614917f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4359 :tutorial/title "Improper Integrals of Even and Odd Functions"} :step/id #uuid "4ed58d6e-1526-5297-982b-b32247e84e4b" :step/math-academy-id 618353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106955}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106955 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105607 :question/id #uuid "705ea272-efb2-54f4-b527-c1d4305e0c6c" :question/math-academy-id "e-514"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320877}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"} :step/id #uuid "647a58a8-d8cb-5567-85af-21033f2a72c6" :step/math-academy-id 618354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106956}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106956 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105609 :question/id #uuid "aad39658-8d3f-5893-b7dd-603b62064c5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-516"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048946}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332975}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"} :step/id #uuid "f45b6067-3889-5762-becb-da9ab2269834" :step/math-academy-id 618357}] :activity/title "Improper Integrals Over the Real Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106007}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101837} :activity/id #uuid "6f288c2e-8f4d-5306-9c16-f6582046db0f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049040 :topic/id #uuid "d56ad601-79c5-5201-9340-f03bd4da4f1f" :topic/math-academy-id 1262} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101931 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x)$ for the piecewise function $f(x)$ given below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ 2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=1 \\\\ -(x-1)^{3}+3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by sketching a graph of the function:\n\n![](images/01/01e1704152432a01a1491f6d7575ada56907272e53f5eeee8a90b8bc96f3eb51.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x) = x^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-(x-1)^3+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}-(x-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =-(1-1)^{3}+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist: \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} f(x) = \\text{DNE}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "80db4900-d9c5-5900-b975-5c26b8f713e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 236 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d1483e1b-2de0-5e5b-a8d1-f4d81897891d" :step/math-academy-id 424 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100977 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100753 :question/id #uuid "62d73be9-5f67-5f44-a45a-f5c25a4dfe27" :question/math-academy-id "e-64"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0995bfd4-20b1-5f12-9d1d-fcb180a6291e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050454}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362398}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Coincide"} :step/id #uuid "51e2792d-7ab3-57f6-a8aa-4475d65fedf7" :step/math-academy-id 425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100978 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100754 :question/id #uuid "e9a033d1-eb3f-5e7e-8237-346b03681499" :question/math-academy-id "e-65"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdd4a2e4-b0e6-54e8-972c-3dc194ce4ab3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392101}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the One-Sided Limits of a Piecewise Function when the Limits Do Not Coincide"} :step/id #uuid "e1dad1df-a9ec-5939-84eb-1443d3e7f165" :step/math-academy-id 428 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101932 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)$ for the piecewise function given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an idea of the behavior of the function around $x=0,$ we can plot the function.\n\n![](images/78/78a832c65a4bb5862694717f52704a61660bd81a34eb7a72cfeeb98a52bb50f1.png)\n\nTo compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3^{x+1}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{3}^{x+1} \\\\  & ={3}^{0+1} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt {x^2+9}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe left and right-sided limits both exist and are equal. So, the overall limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x) =3.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "416b850c-1c8d-5794-a787-3a59d48d0616" :tutorial/math-academy-id 239 :tutorial/title "Limits of Piecewise Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f2bafd4a-88b6-500a-b45d-6de37cdf6804" :step/math-academy-id 430 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100979 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100758 :question/id #uuid "7f534444-765b-5c3a-a381-883a48911700" :question/math-academy-id "e-66"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc794c7-cd0c-5ebc-aa11-9b82e72905fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402495}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits of Piecewise Functions with a Gap in the Domain"} :step/id #uuid "9a5952ff-0a94-5306-8005-3e5b53635c33" :step/math-academy-id 431 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101842}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100980 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100761 :question/id #uuid "baed0974-c209-5e29-99b5-d7f1d55b296d" :question/math-academy-id "e-67"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362399}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"} :step/id #uuid "bcca1c4b-c754-54c1-a87f-df2f0b306855" :step/math-academy-id 433}] :activity/title "Limits of Piecewise Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101099}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097518} :activity/id #uuid "3b059c68-9dd5-5f61-a9e4-d2768be710f4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058 :topic/id #uuid "d6879e2a-6593-5a32-bdad-609e580b017a" :topic/math-academy-id 1048} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097841 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/67/673ea82819858b9a46e0e351a8d6f41b069ed039ee0d31a382110f938f7ef72a.svg)\n\nWe can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination algorithm. The algorithm that we use is very similar to the case of $2 \\times 2$ systems. \n\nOur goal is to create a descending \"staircase\" of $1$'s with $0$'s below, and we achieve this using elementary row operations. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix is now in row echelon form, and we can apply back substitution to solve the original system of equations.\n\n**Note:** The row echelon form of a matrix is *not* unique.  In the previous example, after the second row operation we arrived at\n\n![](images/35/35627a034b30736abb057c01f3251fc047dce780a8cbcf389f8b5c2c2317d766.svg)\n\nThis augmented matrix is already in row echelon form! However, it is always useful to do an extra step and obtain $1$'s along the main diagonal, like we did previously:\n\n![](images/32/32a252e05e299a206f2f3776a2a04419f21726e1f8f542cfd4f328dac3251f21.svg)\n\nHaving $1$'s along the main diagonal makes the final back substitution process a little easier." :tutorial/id #uuid "5d8cb1c1-9e09-560f-83ad-7c8a3d0a78cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3155 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "54dae6c6-bb01-588b-b012-dc097a5b2c65" :step/math-academy-id 17196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097842 :tutorial/content "The **standard Gaussian elimination** for a $3 \\times 3$ system can be described as follows:\n\n- If $a_{11} \\neq 0$ and $a_{11} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1/a_{11}.$\n\n- We use first row and the necessary row operations to make the remaining entries of the first column below $a_{11}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n- if $a_{21} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-a_{21})R_1;$\n\n- if $a_{31} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3 + (-a_{31})R_1.$\n\n- If $a_{22} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2/a_{22}.$\n\n- We use the second row and the necessary row operation to make the entry below $a_{22}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n- if $a_{32} \\neq 0,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3 + (-a_{32})R_2.$\n\n- If $a_{33} \\neq 1,$ then we use the row operation $R_3:=R_3/a_{33}.$\n\n- In some exceptional cases, such as when the element on the diagonal is equal to $0,$ we first need to exchange rows. Then proceed using the same steps as above.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to row echelon form." :tutorial/id #uuid "955c2b9a-3d5f-52c3-b4f3-62541732ffcc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3156 :tutorial/title "General Algorithm"} :step/id #uuid "4f927453-918f-5476-ba92-06cc1a2e3f72" :step/math-academy-id 17197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096672 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096459 :question/id #uuid "b77c065c-3821-513c-9376-6e7108074616" :question/math-academy-id "e-4701"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395769}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "8c5555a5-f06e-5c31-9de6-ec1aef27abbb" :step/math-academy-id 17198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097521}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096460 :question/id #uuid "7760b6ce-91fb-5ba6-beb2-c244d6a73d69" :question/math-academy-id "e-4702"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd47a43f-1e99-5909-aa48-da8690025197" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414031}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination with Row Exchanges"} :step/id #uuid "813ae7dd-cdf7-5b02-ad5e-3911c5f08ece" :step/math-academy-id 17199}] :activity/title "Solving 3x3 Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096856}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116024} :activity/id #uuid "11d81055-02fc-51f2-be9a-1f28290f6d86" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049220 :topic/id #uuid "e46861d7-4b7a-5813-a6e8-914206606355" :topic/math-academy-id 1565} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116463 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sec x={\\sqrt{2}}, \\qquad  0^\\circ\\leq x <   360^\\circ.\n$$\n\nMost calculators don't have a $\\textrm{arcsec}{x}$ button, so we can't get a principal value immediately. However, by rewriting the equation in terms of $\\cos{x}$, we can get a principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}x & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual. Our principal value is\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right)= 45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1=45^\\circ.$ It lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/c7/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\nx_2 = 360^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 315^\\circ .\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=45^\\circ, 315^\\circ.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0fd2eba9-8794-5210-bea2-d726a0af4875" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2564 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "646dc860-2777-5e44-aa90-555d9b22ef5e" :step/math-academy-id 11842 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116025}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115179 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115008 :question/id #uuid "0d6c1aa8-a2d1-5104-8e27-686c7dd16808" :question/math-academy-id "e-3527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345226}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Special Angles"} :step/id #uuid "f3690006-d413-5d43-b44b-7db5a3b33035" :step/math-academy-id 11843 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116026 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116464 :tutorial/content "We can solve an equation involving secant over any desired domain. For example, suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sec x = 2, \\qquad -2\\pi < x \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nWe can proceed as usual. Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)= \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${\\color{red}x_1}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$ It is in the first quadrant and has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](images/31/318eba038feb091d6e3adb80ddf325de6039bb331a7713cf1e2c02171a6388d4.png)\n\nSince the second solution $\\color{blue}x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x_2} = 2\\pi - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nHowever, we're not done yet. The domain $-2\\pi < x \\leq 2\\pi$ is larger than one period, so there are more than two solutions.\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the desired domain, we need to find angles that are coterminal with ${\\color{red}x_1}$ or ${\\color{blue}x_2}.$ So, we subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until all possible solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nx_3 & = {\\color{red}x_1} -2\\pi = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}- 2\\pi= -\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3},\\\nx_4 &= {\\color{blue}x_2} -2\\pi = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}- 2\\pi= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.}\n$$\n\nAdding or subtracting any more multiples of $2\\pi$ would result in solutions outside the desired domain. So, we have found all the solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are  $x = \\pm \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\pm \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b5492203-ceec-581f-aede-beedabd8c432" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2565 :tutorial/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"} :step/id #uuid "4e4240a5-e7ad-5cb0-a15b-ebc1ba39e102" :step/math-academy-id 11845 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116027}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115180 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115009 :question/id #uuid "62e4b12a-19ed-5316-a395-b67da1313687" :question/math-academy-id "e-3529"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390585}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"} :step/id #uuid "55d39539-f0e5-5f00-8a75-baf3d28ca7c7" :step/math-academy-id 11846 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115181 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115010 :question/id #uuid "cc5f8fe7-fd1c-52f9-9dfd-db9ed0a2d973" :question/math-academy-id "e-3532"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049075}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403864}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "ece91cba-f736-53a4-bb3d-2c7780d95b44" :step/math-academy-id 11855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115220 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115050 :question/id #uuid "a3b5986b-17df-5d36-aa1f-6d7d0ef35c85" :question/math-academy-id "e-4260"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399057}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles"} :step/id #uuid "db0f19f1-4116-5327-a065-459459f29588" :step/math-academy-id 15614 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116026}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116484 :tutorial/content "Are there any solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} = \\dfrac{1}{5}, \\qquad  0 \\leq x < 2\\pi?\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\sec{x}= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = 5.\n$$\n\nThis equation has no solution because we always have $-1 \\leq \\cos{x} \\leq 1,$ and the number $5$ is outside of this range. Therefore, $\\sec{x} = \\dfrac{1}{5}$ has no solution.\n\nIn general, when $-1 < a < 1,$ there are no values of $x$ such that $\\sec x = a.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d03c5da6-6a4c-543e-96e9-e7ff27b4ce2c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2896 :tutorial/title "Equations Involving the Secant Function With No Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "9465725a-2525-53da-934d-4381143156f7" :step/math-academy-id 15616 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116028}}] :activity/title "Elementary Trigonometric Equations Containing Secant" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115411}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092995} :activity/id #uuid "95d8fd9f-fddb-5a11-8670-194ad28cdab0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049392 :topic/id #uuid "f1031f6b-01e9-5ea8-af71-27bacc9d53b8" :topic/math-academy-id 1023} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093403 :tutorial/content "We know that we can write a system of three linear equations in three variables in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n- $A$ is the $3\\times 3$ coefficient matrix,\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}$ is the column vector of variables, and\n\n- $\\mathbf{b}$ is the column vector of constants.\n\nIf the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ by \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ {I}_{3}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nLet's see how this works for a particular system in the next example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c0fe954-2cf5-5eb6-b58f-c6963f5be7f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3054 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8e9e60d9-ca5d-5380-9aad-e2eb767f920e" :step/math-academy-id 16484 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092996}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092185 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092025 :question/id #uuid "2737e6bc-5372-50a0-bf2f-59a91f571a57" :question/math-academy-id "e-4520"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12637b1d-ca03-568f-b1fc-6a626eec88de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393884}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Given the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e317b6ac-3382-5ce2-962a-936c0a5dc928" :step/math-academy-id 16485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092997}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092186 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092026 :question/id #uuid "d32f2604-fc3f-5e80-8085-483e6fd9a3ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-4521"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad1721d-26a4-5459-bc81-297ff0e4d678" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049043}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415690}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations By Calculating the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "fbf465d4-96d6-52f9-879f-09f247f27b80" :step/math-academy-id 16486 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092187 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092027 :question/id #uuid "06a50eb4-4976-55fd-aa44-6509b27eccb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4604"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d0cda54-4a4b-5255-b375-ce5e7b604dff" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403954}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Matrix Equation Using Algebraic Manipulation"} :step/id #uuid "9a3ebe3d-7f48-57e9-995c-5652343c5b4a" :step/math-academy-id 16759}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Equations Using Inverse Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092406}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415691} :activity/id #uuid "19f27baf-34c3-5e03-bcb6-d2509997eb57" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049392 :topic/id #uuid "f1031f6b-01e9-5ea8-af71-27bacc9d53b8" :topic/math-academy-id 1023} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "546c8e95-b26b-5ff9-83d6-638b399b687a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5}{2},y=9,z=-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415684} {:answer/id #uuid "854c87f7-583e-578a-92c5-d3db59792427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5}{2},y=9,z=\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415685} {:answer/id #uuid "92176233-f61a-5a1f-a96a-0e59d129daf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5}{2},y=-9,z=-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415686} {:answer/id #uuid "922397fb-a6df-5cdc-b3c4-40ed001f749e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-5,y=18,z=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415687} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d344f8-2327-5d63-96e1-0bdf947cb05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-5,y=-18,z=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415684} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7180735-62b0-5917-9dd2-0f45d066402b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2997dd93-b478-5bad-935c-182c80540138" :question/math-academy-id "q-10091" :question/problem "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y+2z=0 \\\\ x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 3x+y+z=-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-1(1-9)+2(1-6) \\\\  & =+8-10 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 9 \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{2},y=9,z=-\\frac{9}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "100a637e-a49a-5d4a-80d2-e88a9e19ca4c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6788781d-31d5-5bd7-b27b-68afaa4d9325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=B\\,d\\,A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346254} {:answer/id #uuid "9bded5b5-b83d-570e-9629-cd29113e20b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=B\\,d\\,{A}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346708} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a497d7-ea8a-5a5f-b4fd-1d8c483ff5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x={A}^{-1}B\\,d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346783} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d2cc68-86a2-5604-8401-931a07c9e6b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=BA\\,d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346945} {:answer/id #uuid "e304282a-e8e7-5d48-b8b7-fb2bbc3eab5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=AB\\,d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347347}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347347} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4e01459-2873-55b9-8f7c-c4fb473002de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5f0cd9e3-58c9-5ff5-838e-38f22a6e88cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-78570" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation ${A}^{-1}\\,x=B\\,d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $7\\times 7$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $7\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying both sides of the equation ${A}^{-1}\\,x=B\\,d$ by $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}\\,x & =B\\,d \\\\ A{A}^{-1}\\,x & =AB\\,d \\\\ {I}_{7}\\,x & =AB\\,d \\\\ x & =AB\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "227ec066-022a-52e9-9829-1a29de29e8db"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53d8f566-865b-5d1d-968f-ffc7aeaaf0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and ${I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403949} {:answer/id #uuid "6898f8cf-ef60-599d-b703-aeb35e14e141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D$ and ${I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403950} {:answer/id #uuid "0329ed6f-dee9-5205-b238-18984ed73972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and $D$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403951} {:answer/id #uuid "a15466de-3c65-5970-8e36-5b25e21ca209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${D}^{-1}$ and ${D}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403952} {:answer/id #uuid "daabc2d4-f4ce-50f8-9f41-a9e7585ac6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D$ and $z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403953}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403949} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf7d7a2-cf8f-5d79-a5a3-2c7fb6abb055" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "863bd08e-01cd-5a69-b3ce-bacc176f8b4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78559" :question/problem "Consider a system of $n$ linear equations in $n$ variables, written in matrix form as $D\\,z=c.$ Here, $D$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $z$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $c$ is the column vector containing the constants. The unique solution of the system can be found using the method shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,z & =c \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,z & =c \\\\ z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c \\\\ z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom and right to left, what are the missing parts of the derivation?" :question/worked-solution "Since $D$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${D}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $D\\,z=c$ by ${D}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D\\,z & =c \\\\ {D}^{-1}D\\,z & ={D}^{-1}c \\\\ {I}_{n}\\,z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c \\\\ z & ={D}^{-1}\\,c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are ${D}^{-1}$ and ${I}_{n}.$"} :step/id #uuid "42e8894e-d466-5fe7-9f98-3286e55d0c47" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415692}}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Equations Using Inverse Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415683}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101712} :activity/id #uuid "bff965cf-703f-5146-bdd5-fa72e097249e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049600 :topic/id #uuid "039ce63b-6491-5354-8c12-08944c6b0221" :topic/math-academy-id 1156} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101976 :tutorial/content "The **binomial theorem** states that for any real numbers $a$ and $b$ and any positive integer $n,$ \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n},\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that \n\n$$\n{n \\choose k} = \\dfrac{n!}{k!\\, (n-k)!  }.\n$$\n\nLet's use the binomial theorem to find the first few terms in the expansion of\n\n$$\n(2+x)^6.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a = 2,$ $b= x,$ and $n=6.$  Therefore, the first three terms of our expansion are given by\n\n$$\n{\\left( {2 + x} \\right)^{6}}   =  \n{6\\choose0}  {\\left( 2 \\right)^{6}}{\\left( x \\right)^0} +  {6\\choose1}  {\\left( 2 \\right)^{6 - 1}}{\\left( x \\right)^1} +  {6\\choose2}  {\\left( 2 \\right)^{6 - 2}}{\\left( x \\right)^2} + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n{6\\choose0} = {\\color{blue}{1}}, \\qquad {6\\choose1} = {\\color{blue}{6}}, \\qquad {6\\choose2} = {\\color{blue}{15}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expansion can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{6} & =(\\frac{6}{0}){(2)}^{6}{(x)}^{0}+(\\frac{6}{1}){(2)}^{6-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\frac{6}{2}){(2)}^{6-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =(1)({2}^{6})(1)+(6)({2}^{5})(x)+(15)({2}^{4})({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =(1)(64)(1)+(6)(32)(x)+(15)(16)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =64+192\\,x+240\\,{x}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "426cf476-6bfd-5054-aac9-8200164a34b5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3388 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "41da2623-7af5-5f79-be76-db13c7501f64" :step/math-academy-id 18912 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101713}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100891 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100717 :question/id #uuid "1fed57b3-b606-5c2b-a707-2c7810dbe5d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5153"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3b5e36-46db-5d34-92e2-eefb7e0e95c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049243}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387609}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms of a Binomial Expansion"} :step/id #uuid "2ce725e9-9f59-5a2d-9d61-fd95ca45d571" :step/math-academy-id 18913 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101801}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100892 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100718 :question/id #uuid "be5dc7d6-ab62-55e2-8c89-4be3d49e816b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5154"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287894}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term"} :step/id #uuid "453c1e8e-a1de-5e5a-88c3-7f39f95701f1" :step/math-academy-id 18914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100954 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100796 :question/id #uuid "c0969f12-0efa-5f9b-880f-781d8800a6f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9775"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29896622-4bcb-5ad2-91d0-47c5911a619e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269100}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms of an Expansion With Negative Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "5075d69a-6b72-5201-872b-77da45eaf068" :step/math-academy-id 36724 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101714}}] :activity/title "Expanding a Binomial Using Binomial Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101025}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106837} :activity/id #uuid "6d587675-28ef-5040-83fe-9aed98369605" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638 :topic/id #uuid "0748b9c2-fae7-5522-9655-db9e6f1d50c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1347} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107140 :tutorial/content "Recall that a random variable $X$ is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A random variable can take on one of many values from a set $S,$ called the range or support of $X.$\n\nA **continuous random variable** is a random variable whose set of possible values $S$ is given by an interval of real numbers.\n\nFor example, the height of a randomly selected person could be modeled as a continuous random variable because its possible values form an interval of real numbers. For modeling purposes, we usually assume that the height of a randomly selected person could be *any number* in the real interval $(0,\\infty),$ although we could also restrict this to a finite interval $[a,b]$ if we wanted.\n\nOn the other hand, the result of rolling a $6$-sided die is *not* a continuous random variable because its possible values are $1,2,3,4,5,6,$ which does *not* correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nPhysical quantities, such as mass, length, area, volume, time, fluid concentration, etc, are usually modeled as continuous variables. \n\n![](images/73/73d1a2c3852734a6221654882ad5421adb565c96c718e91283e5fbc1f26a72c7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "56f14280-d510-530e-9f12-5e21e8dab7e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6769 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8a357dbd-8538-5f7a-a808-d0bb39634099" :step/math-academy-id 41069 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105860 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105444 :question/id #uuid "e7c527c9-2dc1-50b2-bbf6-10c1a9d122dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10837"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ee39554-fd77-5e90-baa8-f9320ffc3398" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369331}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Continuous Random Variables Given in Context"} :step/id #uuid "6786cf48-e665-5cb3-ba1f-c245b903bc5f" :step/math-academy-id 41070 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107141 :tutorial/content "The **probability density function** (or **pdf**) of a continuous random variable $X$ defined over a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ such that the probability that $X$ lies in the interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nThus, the probability that $X$ lies between $a$ and $b$ can be interpreted as the area bounded between $f(x)$ and the $x$-axis over the interval $x\\in [a,b],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6c21e69a0dbd15ce32d18c2e74d8fcab0276e82bc55b354eaa5140adff610e6.png)\n\nIn general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function over a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor example, let the random variable $X$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $6.$ In this case, the pdf of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis pdf tells us that any number between $0$ and $6$ has an equal chance of being selected, and no number outside this interval can be selected. \n\nNote that this is a valid pdf because it's always nonnegative, and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}x]}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability that our randomly selected number is no greater than $3,$ we integrate the pdf over the interval $[0,3]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}x]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 3]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result matches our intuition. Since every number has an equal chance of being selected, and $[0,3]$ is half the length of $[0,6],$ we must have $P(0 \\leq X \\leq 3) = \\dfrac12.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "aeeb80c6-8faa-5653-8729-e6dc2c52a2e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6770 :tutorial/title "Probability Density Functions"} :step/id #uuid "97ac3c7c-a2f7-585b-a8c6-99d14604c5d2" :step/math-academy-id 41073 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107142 :tutorial/content "The probability density function of a continuous random variable $X$ is somewhat analogous to the probability *mass* function of a discrete random variable in the sense that it describes the probability distribution of $X.$ \n\nHowever, unlike the case with discrete random variables, probability density is *not* the same as probability! Instead, we can think of a pdf as a tool for computing probabilities for continuous random variables.  \n\nThere are two counterintuitive yet important points to be aware of when it comes to interpreting a pdf:\n\n- *The probability that a continuous random variable takes on any particular value is always equal to $0.$* \n\nContinuing the previous example, the probability that our random variable takes on the value $X=5$ is equal to\n\n$$\nP(X = 5) = P(5 \\leq X \\leq 5) = \\int_5^5 \\dfrac{1}{6} \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nIn general, for any value $a$ and any continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X = a) = P(a \\leq X \\leq a) = \\int_a^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- *The values of a probability density function may be greater than $1.$* \n\nFor example, let $Y$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ Then, the pdf of $Y$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(y) = 2, \\qquad y \\in \\left[ 0, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right].\n$$\n\nThis pdf tells us that all numbers between $0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ are equally likely. Again, $f(y)$ is nonnegative, and \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1/2} f(y) \\, \\textrm d y = \\int_0^{1/2} 2 \\, \\textrm d y = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(y)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nLastly, note that since the probability that a continuous random variable takes on a particular value is always equal to $0,$ it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of a particular interval. In other words, for any continuous random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X < b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3bac600c-991c-55c0-8e73-72e609d149f5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6771 :tutorial/title "Interpreting Probability Density Functions"} :step/id #uuid "e61652ca-95a2-5028-b7e7-e01a1b6fa9b4" :step/math-academy-id 41074 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105445 :question/id #uuid "295d2025-c958-55e3-a194-5cdb271a510c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10838"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376075}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"} :step/id #uuid "39c447c4-4037-52b1-ab5b-5e15228f5477" :step/math-academy-id 41075 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105454 :question/id #uuid "32131e34-3141-51b3-8d26-6cee481f168c" :question/math-academy-id "e-11037"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369866}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF"} :step/id #uuid "50dd0bf2-ab05-5ddb-8a4b-93e4fad0d2e1" :step/math-academy-id 41931}] :activity/title "Probability Density Functions of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105985}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093260} :activity/id #uuid "0a81722a-49ce-5840-a251-9d8336e53561" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049891 :topic/id #uuid "1bf5a1e3-35e9-581e-b44a-fa6e4c7b4bff" :topic/math-academy-id 932} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093433 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation $\\mathbf R$ by its standard matrix $R,$ given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{R}$ by computing the matrix products, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =R(i)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {j}^{'} & =R(j)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘} & -\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} & \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlotting the vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ and their images $\\mathbf i'$ and $\\mathbf j',$ we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/35/3516ba56a842adb78f1acae9190b65eb1574629e9f3d6bca25854f547f69385f.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation $\\mathbf R$ represented by the matrix $R$ defines a **counterclockwise rotation of $30^\\circ$ about the origin.**\n\nAs usual, we typically refer to rotations in the counterclockwise sense. However, in this case, the transformation $\\mathbf R$ can also be considered a rotation of $360^\\circ-30^\\circ=330^\\circ$ *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "427eca20-6aad-5c52-a545-fc66d808e5b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3546 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3b310d56-7a89-527c-bf59-b2f90684f0c7" :step/math-academy-id 464465 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093261}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093261 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093435 :tutorial/content "The matrix $R_\\theta,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nrepresents a **rotation by an angle $\\theta$ counterclockwise about the origin.**\n\nTo find the matrix representing a rotation by an angle $\\theta$ *clockwise* about the origin, we replace $\\theta$ with $-\\theta$ in the above. This gives\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(-\\theta ) & -\\operatorname{sin}(-\\theta ) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}(-\\theta ) & \\operatorname{cos}(-\\theta )\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "333a7749-1cde-5720-8a91-4a43d26fc0fd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3548 :tutorial/title "General Rotation Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "96b175ec-ed88-5e8d-8030-67a565348dd4" :step/math-academy-id 464466 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093262}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093262 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092344 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092067 :question/id #uuid "fbf1464e-93e7-5f12-92d3-d0c96a37b2be" :question/math-academy-id "e-5459"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "add52a4e-fff5-5cf6-8fe9-85a44063e2a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048712}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394345}] :knowledge-point/title "Rotating a Vector About the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "e6c8ba1d-d40a-5c09-b23f-464fd88b700f" :step/math-academy-id 464467 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093263}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093263 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092345 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092068 :question/id #uuid "135a22d9-d419-5df5-9366-ee4455ff34ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-5460"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea036e3-19cd-572b-a6cb-2fa466c2a399" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050105}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396210}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting a Given Matrix as a Rotation"} :step/id #uuid "c949ec17-7c15-564d-a513-e970b0226b29" :step/math-academy-id 464470 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093264}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093264 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092346 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092069 :question/id #uuid "7309e735-de9b-5d61-8554-c4994f7c9fe9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5461"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2371a64-5e91-5caa-91c0-f6c7ff572c70" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354330}] :knowledge-point/title "Rotating an Object About the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "4c3d8379-0f57-5843-bba4-5e8fe0677978" :step/math-academy-id 464474}] :activity/title "Rotations as Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092431}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116097} :activity/id #uuid "f5bf1856-47b4-5455-be99-90d735981501" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050122 :topic/id #uuid "2eaec2b9-4c03-5efd-b5aa-8e9655cca4f4" :topic/math-academy-id 1724} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116486 :tutorial/content "We have already seen some properties of matrix multiplication. For example, multiplying any $n \\times n$ square matrix $A$ by the identity matrix $I_n$ will leave it unchanged:\n\n$$\nA I_n = I_n A = A.\n$$\n\nAlso, matrix multiplication follows the **distributive laws**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)C & =AC+BC, \\\\ C(A+B) & =CA+CB.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60b5c83f-3d84-5140-80c7-c4640bea28b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2997 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0b9aabff-e92b-5507-9327-e103c1c53a12" :step/math-academy-id 16195 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115226 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115056 :question/id #uuid "32bd6b27-e95d-5de4-941f-309eb0bc61f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4411"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7ad52ce-d408-5390-b91f-c855274f39ea" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051079}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388090}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Distributive Law"} :step/id #uuid "5f6895fd-de2c-5b1d-96fd-486f6bcbc9a7" :step/math-academy-id 16196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116099}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116487 :tutorial/content "If $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are three matrices that are conformable for multiplication, then they follow the **associative law**:\n\n$$\n\\left( AB\\right) C = A\\left( BC\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, when we wish to compute a product $ABC,$ we don't always have to start by multiplying $AB.$ Alternatively, we can start by multiplying $BC,$ and then multiply $A$ by the result. \n\nSometimes rearranging the parentheses might make the computations slightly easier." :tutorial/id #uuid "31c195bb-9066-55bf-ae02-9de81da2b7bb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2998 :tutorial/title "The Associative Law for Matrix Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "e49acfe6-644b-55a4-86c6-154201b20646" :step/math-academy-id 16197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115227 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115057 :question/id #uuid "3b1395b6-49c1-5e0d-ac94-c2d6fc242f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4412"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408070}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"} :step/id #uuid "31661944-ebfa-574b-9783-47e41b70b808" :step/math-academy-id 16198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116119}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115058 :question/id #uuid "7ab158e6-4222-5cab-88a0-85ce74a7d4b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4413"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345117}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "375c5e0d-680a-5465-afbc-b89498f3e772" :step/math-academy-id 16199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116119 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116492 :tutorial/content "In general, matrix multiplication is **not** commutative. In other words, given two general matrices $A$ and $B$ that are conformable for multiplication, we have\n\n$$\nAB \\neq BA.\n$$\n\nHowever, some particular matrices may satisfy this property. For instance, $AI = IA$ (where $I$ is the identity matrix)." :tutorial/id #uuid "4e92439e-2fc7-59ca-beb8-2ace6a7d3e71" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3129 :tutorial/title "Matrix Multiplication Is Not Commutative in General"} :step/id #uuid "c85ef919-914b-57cc-ab1b-19cb7cd36e6e" :step/math-academy-id 17045 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116101}}] :activity/title "Properties of Matrix Multiplication" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115470}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106639} :activity/id #uuid "4aeb9a17-64c0-5bc4-aa52-e694d7a3e9f1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178 :topic/id #uuid "340f0f9d-19b0-5cff-abb5-75d2e7e75aa9" :topic/math-academy-id 1374} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107082 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n![](images/1a/1ac826685473beeb7a7c0ee90465db019d0d5b70b1af562aa3469ae4f0a54007.svg)\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/17/176e4c49ef19bb92d63190dc6674b66ee55b2fdb306293bae65b7f2b2935701d.svg)\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n![](images/a9/a9dd77f0f2ac0f7bd04b5e70fdca3b126d4eb34b0c929caca48c60f138bc644d.svg)\n\nThe **basic variables** are the variables of our system whose coefficients are the pivots of the row echelon form matrix. Let's illustrate the pivots of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/1b/1b776faa6c69553ce04fbddc4e1aa45e18d7390a32ce34d9e0317b2fa83b8752.svg)\n\nThe pivots are\n\n- $m_{11}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $x,$ and\n\n- $m_{22}=1,$ which is the coefficient of $y.$\n\nTherefore, the basic variables are $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe remaining variables are called the **free variables**. We have only one free variable in our system, which is $z$.\n\nFinally, we can describe the **general solution** by finding equations for the basic variables in terms of the free variables. In our case, this means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z$.\n\nTo do this, we apply the back substitution. From the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\ny - 3z = -5\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = -5+3z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-(-5+3z)+z=2\\,⟹\\, & x=-3+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n![](images/e2/e21bb2b83534152d6bf3d1c43efbd301b42717b975f1f9ebaa28ee4bc3ef31bd.svg)\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n![](images/de/de7a613bbbde25e555608ebc419106701d47875e238832eab7533bf87b0f1ce8.svg)\n\nGeometrically, this is the vector equation of a line, and any point on the line is a solution to our original system.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $z$ and find the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the line. For example, if we choose $z=0,$ then the corresponding values are $x=-3$ and $y=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-3,$ $y=-5,$ $z=0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3f5fe61d-1298-5fad-8678-6cd961832dde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3159 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1f1588ba-e038-5ac2-b2f0-cd6b05552c88" :step/math-academy-id 17206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105604 :question/id #uuid "ff82d566-9671-5cd2-9c24-f1ac6047ed64" :question/math-academy-id "e-4707"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d865bfb4-6ff5-577a-a992-c481af8842b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414557}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Singular 3x3 System of Equations with Two Pivots"} :step/id #uuid "a034e288-f201-529a-8654-d39e76a6c29d" :step/math-academy-id 17207 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106641}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105605 :question/id #uuid "b934f229-5082-5e68-887c-6d3da20f173f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4708"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f742e7-89c6-543b-b43a-f41c27f35abf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400611}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Singular 3x3 System of Equations with One Pivot"} :step/id #uuid "f7ed1c9e-e1c6-5316-a15f-2af80c8133da" :step/math-academy-id 17208 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106745}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105809 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105645 :question/id #uuid "bab65b1f-cf10-58ea-be87-3a3999722d1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6202"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f6a868-46fe-5529-a464-b6f3e0243d30" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403731}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Singular 3x3 System of Equations That Has No Solution"} :step/id #uuid "d9f860aa-5447-57ca-9c2e-9e25b9afa207" :step/math-academy-id 23204}] :activity/title "Solving 3x3 Singular Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106001}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106667} :activity/id #uuid "9012f3b0-be7a-525d-829c-8f6b0ce85627" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050231 :topic/id #uuid "37ecf193-e06c-57b6-8b23-f7d295134db0" :topic/math-academy-id 1358} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107088 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors.\n\nLet $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ be a matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$ We can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this transformation, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/61/61fc1f3f1e76ff49b9967399d1b3a3a63bbe9845a59f2b2841af6e14510b2a7a.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation represented by the matrix $T$ multiplies the vector $\\mathbf{i}$ by a factor of $2$ and leaves the vector $\\mathbf j$ unchanged. This type of transformation is called a **stretch in the $\\mathbf x$-direction.** We can also call it a **horizontal stretch.** In this case, we have a **horizontal stretch of scale factor** $\\mathbf 2.$\n\nIn general, a stretch in the $x$-direction of scale factor $k$ with its center at the origin is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2b251d5e-1184-5f44-b4ff-ba8c7a4fa35d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3538 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "24a98178-6804-578c-bcca-7be62c1fba55" :step/math-academy-id 20030 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105764 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105616 :question/id #uuid "ffbe74ea-33e6-5998-8692-ed5d7bd0c30d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5450"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3105e8eb-010f-542b-9e40-12fda6ea00e7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050136}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299628}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Stretch Transformation Given a Vector and its Image"} :step/id #uuid "c1790453-592c-524b-85e4-b8f01da73075" :step/math-academy-id 20032 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105765 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105617 :question/id #uuid "83ef303a-c25c-5cd5-8ab7-2c14cb05d433" :question/math-academy-id "e-5454"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404170}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "07af67d3-64da-5097-8283-afbf2a41a98e" :step/math-academy-id 20038 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106671}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107089 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors.\n\nLet $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ be a matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$ We can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0.5i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this transformation, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/aa/aa646019f6af73e795661060a1a678b5b3be7961d7e60e3ae601d911033382d2.png)\n\nThe transformation represented by the matrix $T$ adds $0.5\\mathbf{i}$ to $\\mathbf j,$ while the vector $\\mathbf i$ remains unchanged. This type of transformation is called a **horizontal shear.** In this case, we have a **horizontal shear with a shear factor of** $\\mathbf{0.5}.$\n\nIn general, a horizontal shear of scale factor $k$ with its center at the origin is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4c09cece-483e-5612-b070-e22441b1fce3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3541 :tutorial/title "Horizontal Shear"} :step/id #uuid "951b2248-fe6b-5eff-813b-d09a530ec2c3" :step/math-academy-id 20039 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106758}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105766 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105618 :question/id #uuid "4d800cd6-01bd-561a-97cb-1bd672c019c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5455"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c36ce626-1562-51f8-b3fd-d7dc7ce8659c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351183}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Represented by a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "d1d718ce-70f6-584e-8355-6694712167b3" :step/math-academy-id 20040 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105767 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105619 :question/id #uuid "043f45e2-97eb-540d-af0a-b04edb9ce60a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5456"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69bac3fa-d409-5c16-87b2-383423e8d754" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394161}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Square Under a Shear or Stretch Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "d6fd3589-d3b4-5da7-894d-cd35f040c1ef" :step/math-academy-id 20043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107101 :tutorial/content "Now, let $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ be a matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$ We can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this transformation, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](images/19/19b1f75508ae7fc0255edcf2821bb26df352f46544add7740957291d9cb8e117.png)\n\nThe transformation represented by the matrix $T$ multiplies the vector $\\mathbf{j}$ by $2$ and leaves the vector $\\mathbf i$ unchanged. This type of transformation is called a **stretch in the $\\mathbf y$-direction.** We can also call this a **vertical stretch**. In this case, we have a **vertical stretch of scale factor** $\\mathbf 2$. \n\nIn general, a stretch in the $y$-direction of scale factor $k$ with its center at the origin is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1e893b6a-17a2-5f2b-8a94-e7237e811fe5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4414 :tutorial/title "Vertical Stretch"} :step/id #uuid "22da4aa0-ee23-5b88-a006-73210316cdce" :step/math-academy-id 27131 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106668}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107102 :tutorial/content "Now, let $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ be a matrix of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T.$ We can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{'} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}]=i+0.5j \\\\ {j}^{'} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following image:\n\n![](images/66/6626afefeea861f7a4d20e251021ed8db264f365051b85b619770293ac638b37.png)\n\nThe transformation represented by the matrix $T$ adds $0.5\\mathbf{j}$ to $\\mathbf i,$ while the vector $\\mathbf j$ remains unchanged. This type of transformation is called a **vertical shear.** In this case, we have a **vertical shear with a shear factor of** $\\mathbf{0.5}.$\n\nIn general, a vertical shear of scale factor $k$ with its center at the origin is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ k & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7ec9a33-b7de-5d47-a628-944e05516d52" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4415 :tutorial/title "Vertical Shear"} :step/id #uuid "403a0824-e79f-5e4b-8f2d-3d571d56e116" :step/math-academy-id 27140 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106669}}] :activity/title "Shear and Stretch as Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105991}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097514} :activity/id #uuid "34909092-851c-5b8f-b289-94633fbc239b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267 :topic/id #uuid "3afe28a0-5051-52a1-a085-a06727c301b8" :topic/math-academy-id 1047} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097840 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following system of linear equations:\n![](images/b6/b6441688c486562e704a29317847ba53ca5130ff6e35c9d4effdea5ede005d3f.svg)\nThe augmented matrix for this system can be written as \n![](images/b4/b45896f12f8195fcb211411a9a2dcd230cee288822e2d37f2b364f6c1c831c6d.svg)\n\nNotice that the matrix is in *row echelon form* (or REF). Whenever the augmented matrix is in row echelon form, we can solve the system using a method called **back substitution**.\n\nBack substitution involves\n\n- starting with the lowest equation (which is already solved for one variable),\n\n- substituting the result into the next-lowest equation to find the solution for another variable, and\n\n- continuing to substitute the result into the next-lowest equation until the values of all the variables have been found.\n\nHere, the third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny -(3) = 2 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = 5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $y=5$ and $z=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx + 2(5) + (3) = 1\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx = -12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-12,$ $y=5,$ and $z=3.$ We can write it in vector form, as follows:\n\n![](images/8b/8bbbabd0167fc73c58c095fb11ed5efb2c2dca63ee90e95717e26f30f0f170e9.svg)\n\n**Note:** The values $x=-12,$ $y=5,$ and $z=3$ together form *one* solution. We say that this system has *one* solution, *not* three solutions. A system with three solutions would have three different solution vectors, whereas here, we only have *one* solution vector." :tutorial/id #uuid "4d84a9a0-94f9-57e2-a8e6-333ba26d72e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3149 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b94c8727-6dff-5c9a-9868-815a8a185978" :step/math-academy-id 17179 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096456 :question/id #uuid "201a713a-83ca-5e31-9a4f-257795d7eb1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4690"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8f8937b-de15-5d4c-aa77-1d45595fc7af" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413953}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "47cad0ff-3b21-5238-b205-26619a15e0b5" :step/math-academy-id 17180 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096457 :question/id #uuid "cc8ce97a-3bda-506f-b9d2-98ac7524583a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4691"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de05090a-2461-514a-971d-b83c7cea124e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341890}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution Given an Augmented Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "4f928835-23b6-530a-b253-305d43df52fa" :step/math-academy-id 17181 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097517}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096671 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096458 :question/id #uuid "7b44089c-8ca7-57e5-b390-7900647e3884" :question/math-academy-id "e-4692"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e51c9a-6bce-5ea6-a9fe-febb09f8754d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341893}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Given an Augmented Matrix Containing Non-Unit Leading Entries"} :step/id #uuid "a07d47d4-76d1-54a2-8790-392a617e65a8" :step/math-academy-id 17182}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Equations Using Back Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096855}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116106} :activity/id #uuid "c73bd47d-c95f-5782-913d-11a66b5cf13b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050463 :topic/id #uuid "4bb17014-060c-5398-ac8a-30c6798d15e9" :topic/math-academy-id 1729} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116490 :tutorial/content "Matrices can be used to represent systems of equations, such as the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y=5 \\\\ 4x\\text{ }-\\text{ }5y=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\times 2$ system like the one above, we put the coefficients of the system into a $2\\times 2$ matrix, called the **coefficient matrix**:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEach row has the coefficients of one of the equations in the system, and each column consists of the coefficients of one particular variable.\nWe also have the $2\\times 1$ column vectors\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b}$ consists of each constant on the right side of the system and $\\mathbf{x}$ contains the variables.\nWith these matrices, we can write our system as $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ or\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can write any system of two equations with two variables in this form.\nJust to check that the above equation is true, let's multiply out the left-hand side to see if it equals the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ 4x\\text{ }-\\text{ }5y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =b\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d49da49a-c3f8-5718-91a9-4adc236155d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3048 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f7a59c72-2c98-5267-942e-326053020128" :step/math-academy-id 16463 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115231 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115062 :question/id #uuid "87bf279f-9f78-54cb-b9c3-e1bf1c5426ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-4505"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce2e21aa-f89d-5d10-b815-c66a2b46ef5c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354631}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a 2x2 System of Equations Using a Matrix Product"} :step/id #uuid "b1ea43d1-62f0-5ea5-b504-4e92139c4783" :step/math-academy-id 16464 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116108}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115232 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115063 :question/id #uuid "359f4b72-60d2-54e2-b7ef-c48f1e3af708" :question/math-academy-id "e-4506"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f746a1-ea9e-5136-90d6-d4dee1306c17" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299619}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a 2x2 System of Equations Using a Matrix Product by Reorganizing"} :step/id #uuid "8395e7bd-de36-5136-8556-30a9d038a483" :step/math-academy-id 16465 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115233 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115064 :question/id #uuid "b87aa5eb-f689-5f34-baeb-f97c3f200552" :question/math-academy-id "e-4507"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2300a1-f31e-5a24-889a-6f45e1e8647b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403495}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Matrix Product as a 2x2 System of Equations"} :step/id #uuid "896b2689-9de5-527f-a925-513c7675ce5c" :step/math-academy-id 16466}] :activity/title "Representing 2x2 Systems of Equations Using a Matrix Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115475}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097476} :activity/id #uuid "2c47673d-00b6-5b99-b927-1887958b60aa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050467 :topic/id #uuid "4bf7862e-8b34-577f-9a7c-1f3cc4989b50" :topic/math-academy-id 1102} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097476 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097831 :tutorial/content "It's often helpful to express a given vector in terms of sums and scalar multiples of a handful of known vectors.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following diagram:\n\n![](images/a6/a6c8dcdf9be32345bbc92ff162992e6c9cf02c107de8ff74caf534b508bfe411.png)\n\nHow can we express the vector $\\overrightarrow{PR}$ in terms of the vectors $\\textbf{a}$ and $\\textbf{b}$ only?\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QR}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c72ba23124e9622b50f9daee4006ca4aac6a460b8a694a368d0e53393a85053.png)\n\nFrom the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{PQ}}}   = \\mathbf{a} \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n{\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{QR}}}  = -\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9d284b2d2683ae43fe57e8c96433803bc908d764e489ac0f861ad978393fde7d.png)\n\nTherefore, by substituting these values into our equation for ${\\color{red}\\overrightarrow{PR}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QR} \\\\  & =\\text{a}+(-\\text{b}) \\\\  & =\\text{a}-\\text{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d92705a2-c9e5-5782-91e1-fa086fa5bffb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2904 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "60458875-ac64-5849-86d5-1d54ffdf12af" :step/math-academy-id 15694 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097477 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096647 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096444 :question/id #uuid "e47a8da7-9276-5f4c-8ee6-1fd737c2e9ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-4277"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f0ec6b-9cb4-5246-9ac6-23b35e3b224e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388869}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of More Than Two Known Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "7edff0ab-8d12-5173-baab-3ba46883ba4d" :step/math-academy-id 15695 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096486 :question/id #uuid "0b0dbbc1-d39d-5aff-9f16-075779d36659" :question/math-academy-id "e-5015"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345292}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"} :step/id #uuid "3c69fb77-271a-5dcc-af1c-f2f7b0d14282" :step/math-academy-id 18415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096603 :question/id #uuid "c7526e18-37a9-572a-a6a3-0384bbccf41d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9934"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c43200-991b-5854-be1d-fa9db80f3725" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049603}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387008}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector as a Sum or Difference of Two Known Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "1d59ceb2-63e4-5d77-913c-73dbfc0ece9e" :step/math-academy-id 37408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097477}}] :activity/title "Problem Solving Using Vector Diagrams" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096882}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097689} :activity/id #uuid "e88ba3cb-c9bf-5fd4-b41a-f7a4a7dbdf65" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050564 :topic/id #uuid "53f1b90b-679a-5fc8-8d88-3abe54c112ec" :topic/math-academy-id 1081} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097810 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with decimal coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.2x+y=1 \\\\ 0.15x-0.04y=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the system easier to solve, we turn it into a system involving integer coefficients only.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by $10,$ or $100,$ or $1000,$ etc. depending on how many decimal places we need to eliminate. In this particular instance:\n\n- We need to eliminate one decimal place in the top equation, so we multiply by $10.$\n\n- We need to eliminate two decimal places in the bottom equation, so we multiply by $100.$\n\nSo, we multiply the first equation by $10$ and the second equation by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.2x+y=1 & |\\times 10 \\\\ 0.15x-0.04y=0.2 & |\\times 100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can solve the system of equations using either substitution or elimination." :tutorial/id #uuid "ca705a2f-5d99-5642-87c9-9aadf1ef0319" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1465 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ea69e83f-e045-5c97-abd8-0852c912ada9" :step/math-academy-id 3426 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097690}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096784 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096368 :question/id #uuid "0a5391bf-06ef-5099-a835-0375ff96c342" :question/math-academy-id "e-1038"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7578c48-cc9e-5170-8ffe-5ebee6580677" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049060}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401798}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Decimals in One Equation"} :step/id #uuid "28926038-4dab-51b2-815e-2a17fa359942" :step/math-academy-id 3427 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097691}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096785 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096369 :question/id #uuid "8f4b321d-ebc7-5f78-9bde-b51b297a0cc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1039"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc62d8df-450f-5f7a-b647-b53c051f8339" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401908}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Decimals in Both Equations"} :step/id #uuid "20367edf-5f5a-5aec-a12b-3f3cf7c4a01e" :step/math-academy-id 3431}] :activity/title "Systems of Linear Equations With Decimal Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096870}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111588} :activity/id #uuid "2bf966de-3fac-57c5-99d5-21cc8c224f16" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050641 :topic/id #uuid "5b7cc53f-24b7-5a92-abb0-e272bef77223" :topic/math-academy-id 1535} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110651 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110535 :question/id #uuid "ae48dbff-25c8-55a9-a9e0-fda0b3ecfd50" :question/math-academy-id "e-7959"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbd52408-5f2f-58c9-9a88-bd992fe8752c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050153}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404468}] :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Strict Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "c956bed0-e7a0-55c2-ba2f-0eeac3a7e01f" :step/math-academy-id 29676 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110652 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110536 :question/id #uuid "4809beec-7c4e-5bd1-989d-1dc54d78d892" :question/math-academy-id "e-7960"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a258070e-5c3c-595e-81d6-3e0f9b9a5daf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404475}] :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Mixed Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "420d1396-a598-5166-9063-80e51d94ab6d" :step/math-academy-id 29678 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110537 :question/id #uuid "88a7aab5-813a-58e2-a695-8ee72dd4efb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7964"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42525832-e186-538f-baf8-bb4418e9820b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049646}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404461}] :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Inequalities Presented in Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "2acd7fcc-ee79-517d-a38d-0a1aeeaa4fda" :step/math-academy-id 29702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111822 :tutorial/content "Just as we can have a system of linear equations, we can also have systems of linear inequalities. For example, the system\n\n![](images/42/422ff3fd1ff0704d34d5d2f098e3c524f409d2a4da7d6fa3d792020bbc8ac56d.svg)\nis solved by the points that satisfy the inequalities ![](images/23/23f2bda2195f51e1cbf95f6905d547dc9004733d89866ae9f92ef046c330417c.svg) *and* ![](images/87/877dcbf886336f3054b3df8910cee950868e2819e007cf11544a6be7f15b81d3.svg) simultaneously.\n\nTo find the points that satisfy both inequalities, we first draw the graph of the inequality ![](images/9a/9a758452dbe8e0ede75fb97296110356c18e46275000ac28ebf631d91fa05b22.svg)\n\n![](images/6f/6f3f7b6eb3109225256c5f4342333d03c19ef31b40d5ae5b24c136950a31f76a.png)\n\nNow, on top of the diagram above, we draw the graph of the second inequality, ![](images/c1/c1e37a6573ce7b419aeff619c181b43325304505c05b669394ef0818ace2555f.svg)\n\n![](images/e0/e017eefa235ac4b95009570c59fe5e5587f50bb02299305cfb9126573c0156bd.png)\n\nThe solution to the system is the *intersection* of the two regions (i.e., the part that is shaded twice).\n\n![](images/c1/c1d9b4358e15ca09e8358f8e298fc0ffa745b885adcd7ea522e07f1d221a2c90.png)\n\nThe important thing to realize is that *any* point in the shaded region above satisfies *both* inequalities." :tutorial/id #uuid "1e005009-6033-5e6b-b50c-c802bbffe3e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1473 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f5b482d0-1628-52e2-8a01-cbff575355b1" :step/math-academy-id 3482 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110658 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110290 :question/id #uuid "4b19b389-7d0e-525b-b37f-787e53f5e3b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-1056"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cd1bb61-2770-5096-83d6-d1e4a7189e9a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050095}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403037}] :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing \"Or Equal To\" Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "712b9963-4a02-5cba-8c6c-337db89072ce" :step/math-academy-id 3483 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111579}}] :activity/title "Systems of Linear Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110879}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116110} :activity/id #uuid "d07d2d5c-d3e0-595b-84ff-e18597a3d040" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906 :topic/id #uuid "70425962-eba3-5483-950c-6e00e8bba0c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1730} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116491 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n- $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n- The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n- The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)." :tutorial/id #uuid "5a0ce34c-f39a-5c24-bab3-840ff974402c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3050 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "836cc4ee-5cd5-58a8-8fc6-487e670dc062" :step/math-academy-id 16471 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115065 :question/id #uuid "c0c08150-98f9-5677-a7a8-390cffc0c5b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4511"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050944}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342015}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"} :step/id #uuid "33db29bf-42ca-5174-b04b-c5529dc2bc98" :step/math-academy-id 16472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116112}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116112 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115066 :question/id #uuid "8c1e5afa-0afa-56ee-bbfa-4e84c64093ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-4512"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412421}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "2a71d8e7-c420-59a3-927f-619b1fbcc37e" :step/math-academy-id 16473 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116113}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115236 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115067 :question/id #uuid "cb19dcff-bb9d-55dc-941b-48153ca62e7a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4513"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412420}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"} :step/id #uuid "72cfa9ea-e123-5f2c-bad3-38a7904ca9f0" :step/math-academy-id 16474}] :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115476}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097454} :activity/id #uuid "955ea517-6a8e-5d79-b982-bb76840b57f8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050926 :topic/id #uuid "725c1842-0d9b-590f-8559-27b6fce12ae4" :topic/math-academy-id 1045} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097919 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with fractional coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y=-4 \\\\ 7x-5y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can turn the system into an equivalent system involving only integer coefficients. This will make it easier to solve.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by its lowest common denominator. In this instance, multiplying the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions $(4)$ will do the job:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y=-4 & |\\,\\times 4 \\\\ 7x-5y=-2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the system using, for example, the elimination method. Notice that the $5y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-5y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\cancel{5y} & =-16 \\\\ 7x-\\cancel{5y} & =-2 \\\\ 9x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=-2$ into the equation $7x - 5y= -2$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-5y & =-2 \\\\ 7(-2)-5y & =-2 \\\\ -14-5y & =-2 \\\\ -5y & =12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2, \\,y=-\\dfrac{12}{5}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68821d79-90e2-580a-95fd-a5268f848ff4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8706 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "703fc6f0-096a-5e00-b1a3-f32d4b4f9ac6" :step/math-academy-id 148710 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097455}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096634 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096394 :question/id #uuid "fb7a4815-45e8-5288-8e96-c3741f1a2097" :question/math-academy-id "e-14732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049060}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332888}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "ef39da0c-015c-59a6-af08-1693a877674f" :step/math-academy-id 148711 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097456}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097456 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096635 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096395 :question/id #uuid "7f77dc95-b22e-51af-a8c2-ef60ae4f3098" :question/math-academy-id "e-14733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345133}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"} :step/id #uuid "e0176cd1-6ae7-5332-9e2d-1cdfde89e84c" :step/math-academy-id 148714 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097457}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097930 :tutorial/content "We can also use the substitution method to solve systems of equations containing fractional coefficients.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we multiply both equations by the lowest common denominator of the fractions in the system.\n\n- Then, we apply the substitution method as usual.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "33f3d90d-d232-500a-b9c6-cecc2546b571" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9138 :tutorial/title "Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "3dad326e-86ed-5c46-93c4-a610d7869f3e" :step/math-academy-id 148717 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096636 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096388 :question/id #uuid "210f48cc-8812-564d-9dae-5a8a42165ef9" :question/math-academy-id "e-14005"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90455606-1080-552e-a825-0cc7054b4be3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292609}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "63a09a62-bd67-54f7-9a93-8dbf670b155c" :step/math-academy-id 148718 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097459}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097459 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096637 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096389 :question/id #uuid "ff844be7-7d1e-5a1f-a4d6-1d4313f5fd2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14006"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c880ac-41a9-543f-b7a1-b4dfcc5de99d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290334}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations"} :step/id #uuid "167c3d0e-c8ed-5974-9e0e-6fe077e49acc" :step/math-academy-id 148721}] :activity/title "Systems of Linear Equations With Fractional Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096853}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092992} :activity/id #uuid "eedb9663-ba7e-5ff2-ad61-597482d1636e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050944 :topic/id #uuid "73f31c1a-1d34-559e-8292-a932c5c83421" :topic/math-academy-id 934} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093402 :tutorial/content "If the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nby \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ {I}_{2}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}=A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor instance, to find the solution to the system of equations\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n we first calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the solution to this system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solution is $x=3$ and $y=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "39b17ee8-086a-5282-a414-c88d455b6107" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3049 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7bdb49ae-e07e-5e55-b869-25f67ee0ac21" :step/math-academy-id 16467 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092993}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092183 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092023 :question/id #uuid "cb7d8dc0-98eb-53d0-9e48-3b1c2d7f6308" :question/math-academy-id "e-4508"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c89778fa-3c8d-5f0a-a0f8-c9b3d9193ffd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394786}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "7e787e4f-33b0-514d-ad4f-569e9dfb525d" :step/math-academy-id 16468 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092184 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092024 :question/id #uuid "422d31ad-8caa-5ce2-a0e0-c905fcc2640d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4509"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4691976a-d825-5f6c-aed7-0d9b45560952" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393215}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix With Reorganization"} :step/id #uuid "696d1030-ccb5-5376-b23e-62d38e346070" :step/math-academy-id 16469 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093404 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=3 \\\\ 6x+3y=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCan we use the matrix method of $\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}$ to find a solution to this system?\n\nThis system can be represented in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo use this matrix method, we need to find $A^{-1}.$ However\n\n$$\n\\det (A) = 2\\cdot 3 - 1\\cdot 6 =0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is not invertible. So we cannot use the matrix method to find a solution to the system.\n\nIn general, if the coefficient matrix $A$ is not invertible, there is no unique solution. This means that either\n\n- a solution may not exist, or\n\n- there may be infinitely many solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "e5e2bff7-df5b-5628-92e3-a50ca8dd6be8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3066 :tutorial/title "Systems of Equations Where the Coefficient Matrix is Singular"} :step/id #uuid "ff58cfed-22a0-501b-8983-7edc00d85964" :step/math-academy-id 16614}] :activity/title "Solving 2x2 Systems of Equations Using Inverse Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092433}]}
